    bio: "Jean-Jacques Dessalines was born into slavery in the French colony of Saint-Domingue around 1758, likely in the northern province near Grande-Rivière-du-Nord. He bore on his body the literal marks of enslavement — scars from the whip that he reportedly refused to conceal in later life, wearing them as testimony rather than shame. He joined the revolutionary movement in its early stages and rose rapidly through the military hierarchy, proving himself a commander of exceptional boldness and an organiser of fierce efficiency. He became one of Toussaint Louverture\'s most trusted lieutenants, learning the art of irregular warfare in the mountains and sugar fields of the island.\n\nAfter Toussaint\'s capture and deportation by the French in 1802, Dessalines emerged as the dominant military figure of the revolution. Where Toussaint had been a diplomat as well as a soldier, Dessalines was above all a fighter — he understood the war in absolute terms and prosecuted it accordingly. His strategic decision to avoid pitched battle against the French regulars while waiting for yellow fever to decimate them showed a remarkable grasp of asymmetric warfare. As the epidemic destroyed Leclerc\'s army and then Rochambeau\'s, Dessalines pressed his advantage with increasing confidence, ultimately forcing the surrender of French forces at Cap-Français in November 1803.\n\nOn the first of January 1804, Dessalines proclaimed the independence of Haiti — the name drawn from the indigenous Taíno word for the island — becoming the new nation\'s first ruler, initially as Governor-General for Life. Later that year he declared himself Emperor Jacques I, a title evidently influenced by the imperial ambitions sweeping the Atlantic world in Napoleon\'s wake. His reign was brutal by any measure: he ordered the massacre of the majority of the remaining white population of Haiti in the spring of 1804, reasoning, with a terrible logic, that their presence would always provide a pretext for French recolonisation. The act appalled the world and ensured Haiti\'s diplomatic isolation for decades.\n\nDessalines was assassinated in October 1806 by rivals in his own officer class, ambushed and killed on the road to Port-au-Prince. He was not yet fifty. The revolution he had helped lead and finally concluded remains one of the most extraordinary events in human history: the only successful slave revolt to produce an independent nation state. His legacy is contested — hero and tyrant in equal measure — but his achievement is not. Against the greatest military empire of the age, aided by a disease he did not know existed, he won."
